#### P0-3 · Terminate AudioWorklet processors on module delete (Faust destroy + shared lifecycle) [U — stability#1; verifier did not rule — re-verify the greps before building]
- **What:** Two phases. **Phase 1 (S):** call `FaustAudioWorkletNode.destroy()` in the ~15 Faust module disposes (or centralize in faust-runtime's returned handle) — faustwasm's destroy() closes the port and makes process() return false (faustwasm esm/index.js:529-531, 621-625). **Phase 2 (L):** shared `lib/worklet-lifecycle` — port message `{type:'dispose'}` → processor frees state and `return !this.disposed`; wire into every own-code handle dispose.
- **Evidence:** 136 unconditional `return true` across packages/dsp/src, zero lifecycle port messages, zero production `.destroy()` call sites; module disposes only stop+disconnect (adsr.ts:157-166, reverb.ts:85-89); Web Audio lifetime rules keep a `return true` processor alive for the context's lifetime regardless of connections. Preset-slot switches rebuild the whole patch against the SAME context → 20-module patch × N switches = 20N orphaned processors.
- **Expected win:** Flat (instead of monotonic) audio-thread CPU/memory across a session with deletes/slot switches; removes a whole underrun-compounding class for long live sets.
- **Effort:** Phase 1 S · Phase 2 L.
- **Risk + gates:** Phase 1: post-dispose setParam on a closed port → try/catch; web-package only, **no ART churn**. Phase 2 touches all 69 worklet sources → re-pins ~48 ART `.sha` files (audio unchanged — re-pin LAST, confirm only `.sha` moved, per art-sha-pin-regenerate-last) + must respect the no-top-level-export worklet rule. Unit-pin: disposed processor's process() returns false.
- **Measure:** `chrome://tracing` audio thread CPU% + a debug live-processor counter, over a scripted 10× add-20-modules/delete-all soak; DevTools heap snapshot of the AudioWorkletGlobalScope before/after.

